    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most homeowners think of surges as lightning, but the bigger daily threat comes from inside: air conditioners, well pumps, garage door openers and furnace blowers switching on and off, and utility switching on the grid. A panel-mounted surge protective device is a small box costing roughly $60 to $300 that clamps those spikes before they spread through every circuit. For new and replaced services, the electrical code now requires one.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 6 Best Whole House Surge Protectors, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Devices are ranked on their UL 1449 listing type, surge current rating, voltage protection rating, status indication, mounting options and warranty terms as published by each manufacturer, plus recurring owner and electrician feedback. No surge testing was done for this list; every number is attributed to the spec sheet.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="how-surges-happen" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Where Household Surges Actually Come From
          </h2>

          <p>
            A surge is a short spike in voltage, lasting millionths to thousandths of a second, well above the normal 120 or 240 volts. Lightning produces the dramatic ones, but direct strikes are rare. Far more common are nearby strikes that induce surges into overhead lines, and utility switching events such as capacitor banks coming online or a transformer fault elsewhere on the grid. Those external events can send thousands of volts into a service.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inside the home, motors and compressors are the main source. Every time an air conditioner, heat pump, refrigerator, well pump or furnace blower shuts off, the collapsing magnetic field in its motor kicks a small spike back into the wiring. Each one is modest, often a few hundred volts, but they happen many times a day, year after year. Industry estimates commonly put internally generated surges at the majority of all events in a typical home.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those small, repeated spikes do not destroy electronics in one blow. They wear down the sensitive components in control boards, power supplies and LED drivers over time, which shows up as appliances failing early with no obvious cause. Modern homes are full of these boards: furnaces, washing machines, ranges, refrigerators, garage door openers, smart thermostats and every LED fixture.
          </p>

          <p>
            A whole-house surge protector does not make a home lightning-proof. Nothing at the panel can stop a direct strike from damaging a house. What it does is divert the frequent, moderate surges to ground before they spread through every branch circuit, which protects hardwired appliances that cannot be plugged into a surge strip. That is where the value is.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE HONEST EXPECTATION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A panel surge protector reduces wear and damage from everyday spikes. It does not stop a direct lightning strike.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Think of it as protection for the HVAC board, the range, the washer and the furnace, things you cannot plug into a power strip. Pair it with point-of-use protection for the most sensitive electronics.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="which-homes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Which Homes Benefit Most
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every home with modern appliances benefits from panel protection, but some benefit more. Homes served by long overhead lines in rural areas see more induced surges from lightning and utility switching than homes on short underground feeds in a subdivision. Regions with frequent thunderstorms, such as the Southeast and the Gulf Coast, see far more lightning activity than the Pacific Northwest. If you live in one of those situations, choose a device toward the top of the capacity range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Homes with well pumps, heat pumps, central air, electric vehicle chargers and solar inverters carry more expensive electronics and more internal surge sources. A variable-speed heat pump or a modern inverter-driven mini-split has a control board that can cost several hundred dollars to replace, and the motors themselves generate spikes. Protecting those circuits at the panel is cheap insurance against early failures.
          </p>

          <p>
            Older homes are a special case. If the panel is a brand with known reliability problems, or the wiring has no equipment ground, a surge protector cannot do its job well, because it relies on a solid path to ground. In that situation, fix the grounding and consider a panel upgrade first, then add the surge device as part of the work. An electrician can tell you in one visit whether the panel and grounding are sound.
          </p>

          <p>
            Homes that lose power often should think about surges at restoration. When the grid comes back after an outage, voltage can swing as loads return all at once. Some homeowners switch off major appliance breakers during an outage and turn them back on a few minutes after power returns. A panel surge device helps with those swings, and a <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station</a> or generator plan covers the outage itself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="spd-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Type 1, Type 2 and Type 3 Devices
          </h2>

          <p>
            Surge protective devices are listed under <strong>UL 1449</strong>, and the listing assigns a type based on where the device may be installed. A <strong>Type 1</strong> device can go ahead of the main breaker, including at the meter or in a meter-main combination, and does not rely on an overcurrent device. It is built to handle larger external surges and is often used where the service is exposed to lightning.
          </p>

          <p>
            A <strong>Type 2</strong> device goes downstream of the main breaker, typically landing on a double-pole breaker inside the panel. This is the most common whole-house protector, and it is what most homeowners install. Many current products are listed as Type 1 and Type 2, so they can be installed either way, which gives an electrician flexibility.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Type 3</strong> devices are point-of-use protectors: plug-in strips and receptacle-style protectors at least 10 meters of wire from the panel. They catch surges generated inside the home and any let-through that passes the panel device. A good protection plan uses a Type 1 or 2 device at the panel and Type 3 devices at the most sensitive electronics.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a format question. Most panel devices are small boxes mounted to the outside of the panel through a knockout, wired to a double-pole breaker. Some, like breaker-style units from panel makers, snap into two breaker slots inside the panel. These are easier to install but limited to panels from that maker, and they consume breaker spaces that may be scarce in a full panel.
          </p>

