        <div id="spec-sheet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Reading the Spec Sheet
          </h2>

          <p>
            The biggest number on the box is the <strong>surge current rating</strong>, given in kiloamps, such as 50 kA, 80 kA or 140 kA. It describes the largest single surge the device can absorb, or in some marketing the total across all modes. Higher ratings mean more headroom and generally a longer life, since each surge degrades the internal components slightly. For most homes, anything from about 50 kA up is adequate; higher ratings add longevity and margin.
          </p>

          <p>
            More useful is the <strong>voltage protection rating</strong>, or VPR, which is the let-through voltage measured under a standard test surge. Lower is better: a VPR of 600 or 700 volts lets less energy through to equipment than one of 1,200 volts. Compare VPR for the line-to-neutral mode, which matters most for 120-volt equipment. Some brands bury this on the spec sheet, which is a reason to look.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, check the <strong>nominal discharge current</strong>, or In, which is 10 kA or 20 kA for most residential devices. Type 1 devices are tested at 10 or 20 kA; a 20 kA In rating indicates a more robust device. <strong>MCOV</strong>, the maximum continuous operating voltage, should suit a 120/240-volt split-phase service, which nearly all residential devices do. Also check the <strong>short-circuit current rating</strong> matches or exceeds what the panel is rated for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, look at the protection modes and indicators. Good devices protect hot to neutral, hot to ground, neutral to ground and hot to hot. Status LEDs show when protection is still active, and some devices add an audible alarm or a surge counter. Because the internal metal oxide varistors wear out silently, a clear indicator is the only way to know when it is time to replace the unit.
          </p>

