        <div id="code-requirements" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What the Electrical Code Requires
          </h2>

          <p>
            The 2020 edition of the National Electrical Code added section <strong>230.67</strong>, which requires a surge protective device on all services supplying dwelling units. The rule applies to new services and to services being replaced. The device must be a Type 1 or Type 2 SPD, installed in or next to the service equipment, and it must be an integral part of the equipment or located immediately adjacent to it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Adoption depends on where you live. States and municipalities adopt NEC editions on their own schedules, and some amend sections or delay them. Most US jurisdictions now enforce the 2020 or 2023 code, but a few still run on older editions. If you are upgrading a panel or service, the permit and inspection will almost certainly require an SPD in jurisdictions on the newer codes.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 2023 NEC kept the requirement and extended it to other sleeping occupancies, such as dormitory units, guest rooms and nursing home patient sleeping rooms. For an existing home with an older panel and no planned service work, the code does not require you to add a surge protector. It is still a good idea, and the installation must meet the code in force when the work is done.
          </p>

          <p>
            Codes also cover how the device is wired. Manufacturers specify the breaker size, usually a double-pole 20 to 50 amp, and conductor gauge. They also stress short lead lengths: every extra foot of wire between the device and the bus adds let-through voltage. Electricians mount the device as close as possible to the breaker it lands on, keep conductors straight and avoid coiling the excess.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Installation and Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Installing a panel surge protector means working inside the service panel. Even with the main breaker off, the service conductors and the lugs ahead of the main breaker remain energized by the utility, and contact with them can kill. Panel work also requires a permit in most jurisdictions. For those reasons, this guide recommends hiring a licensed electrician unless you are qualified and permitted to do panel work yourself.
          </p>

          <p>
            A typical Type 2 install takes an electrician under an hour. The power is shut off at the main breaker, the panel cover removed, a knockout opened close to an available double-pole breaker position, and the device mounted with a short nipple. The line conductors land on the breaker, the neutral on the neutral bar and the ground on the ground bar, all kept as short and straight as possible. The panel is then closed and the device's status lights checked.
          </p>

          <p>
            Breaker space can be a problem in older or full panels. If there are no open spaces for a double-pole breaker, an electrician may use tandem breakers where the panel listing allows them, or mount a Type 1 device at the meter where the utility permits. Breaker-style surge devices need two adjacent spaces in a compatible panel. Find the panel label before you buy anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            Good grounding is essential. A surge protector diverts energy to the grounding electrode system, so a poor ground connection reduces its effectiveness. Ask the electrician to confirm the grounding electrode conductor and bonding are sound. Also bond cable TV, satellite and phone lines at the same intergrounding point, so a surge on those lines cannot create a voltage difference across connected equipment. If you plan to label circuits while the panel is open, a <a href="/best-circuit-breaker-finder/">circuit breaker finder</a> speeds the job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="layered-protection" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Layered Protection Works Best
          </h2>

          <p>
            A panel surge protector reduces surges to a level that is still too high for sensitive electronics. Its let-through voltage, typically 600 to 1,000 volts, is far better than the thousands that could otherwise arrive, but computers, televisions and networking gear benefit from a second layer. That is the role of plug-in Type 3 protectors near the equipment.
          </p>

          <p>
            The two layers complement each other. The panel device handles the large external surges, so the point-of-use strip is not overwhelmed and wears out more slowly. The strip handles internal surges from appliances on the same circuit and anything that slips past the panel. Choose strips with a UL 1449 listing, a low VPR and an indicator light, and replace them every few years or after a major event.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protect the other entry points too. Coaxial, phone and Ethernet cables can carry surges straight into a television, modem or router. Some panel devices include coax and phone protection modules, and many point-of-use strips include coax and Ethernet pass-throughs. Generators and solar inverters are further entry points that should be grounded and protected per their installation instructions.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you run a portable generator through a transfer switch or interlock during outages, a surge protector at the panel provides some protection from generator voltage swings, but it is not a substitute for a clean inverter generator for sensitive loads. Our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator guide</a> covers generator choice and safe connection. And whatever surge plan you choose, keep water treatment controls, well pumps and UV systems on protected circuits, as described in our <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/">home water filtration guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What It Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            The devices themselves are inexpensive. At the time of writing, compact Type 2 units cost roughly $50 to $120, mid-range devices with higher kA ratings and better indication roughly $100 to $200, and premium Type 1 and 2 units with surge counters or outdoor enclosures roughly $150 to $350. Breaker-style devices from panel makers usually fall in the $60 to $150 range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Installation by a licensed electrician typically adds roughly $150 to $500, depending on local labor rates, the permit fee and how easy the panel is to work in. If the electrician is already on site for another job, adding a surge protector often costs much less. Doing it during a panel upgrade is the cheapest time of all, since the code may require it anyway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare that with what is at risk. A furnace or heat pump control board costs roughly $200 to $600 to replace with labor, a refrigerator main board roughly $150 to $400, and a front-load washer board similar. One saved board pays for the device and install. Many manufacturers also offer connected-equipment warranties, but read the terms carefully, as claims typically require proof of proper installation and grounding.
          </p>

          <p>
            Homeowner's insurance may cover surge damage to appliances, but deductibles often exceed the cost of a single board, and repeated claims can raise premiums. Treating the surge protector as a way to avoid small claims altogether is the more realistic way to think about its value. A few insurers offer discounts or inspection credits for whole-house protection, so it is worth asking your agent.
          </p>

