        <div id="lifespan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Lifespan and Replacement
          </h2>

          <p>
            The protective components in most devices, metal oxide varistors, lose a little capacity every time they absorb a surge they absorb. After enough events, they lose the ability to clamp, and the device stops protecting while the house keeps working normally. Manufacturers often suggest a service life in the range of 5 to 10 years, but the real answer depends on how often and how hard your service gets hit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the status lights at least twice a year, for instance when you test smoke alarms. A green light usually means protection is active; a dark or red light means one or more modes have failed. Devices with audible alarms or surge counters make this easier. After a nearby lightning strike or a major utility event, check right away.
          </p>

          <p>
            Replacement is the same job as installation. The electrician turns off the breaker, disconnects the old device and fits a new one. If you replace an old device, take the chance to upgrade to a higher kA rating or a better VPR, and confirm the breaker and conductor sizes match the new device's instructions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some devices have replaceable modules, which can be swapped without removing the whole housing. These cost more up front but simplify service. For most homes, a standard sealed device replaced every several years is simpler and cheaper over time, provided someone actually looks at the indicator lights.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep a simple record. Write the install date and model number on a label inside the panel door, next to the circuit directory, and note it again whenever the device is replaced. Insurance adjusters and future electricians both find that useful, and it removes the guesswork about whether a ten-year-old device is still worth trusting. If the house is sold, the next owner will know what protection is in place and when it was last renewed.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you ever see scorch marks, a bulging housing or a burning smell near the device, switch off its breaker and call an electrician. A device that has absorbed a very large surge can fail short, and the breaker is there to disconnect it safely. That is also why the manufacturer's breaker size matters: an oversized breaker may not trip when it should.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating it as lightning-proofing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A panel surge protector diverts common surges but cannot stop a direct lightning strike, which can jump gaps and damage wiring anywhere in the house. Expect it to protect against everyday spikes and nearby strikes. If your area sees frequent lightning, talk to a specialist about a lightning protection system.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Long, coiled lead wires</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every extra foot of wire between the device and the breaker adds let-through voltage and weakens protection. Mount the device right next to the breaker it connects to, trim conductors short and keep them straight. Coiling excess wire inside the panel is a common amateur error that undermines the whole purchase.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the indicator lights</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Surge protectors fail silently. The house keeps working while the device no longer clamps anything. Check the status lights twice a year and after storms, and replace the unit when an indicator goes out. A device with an audible alarm or surge counter makes this easier.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping point-of-use protection</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The panel device lets through several hundred volts, which is still too much for sensitive electronics, and it does nothing about surges generated inside the house on the same circuit. Use UL 1449 listed plug-in protectors for computers, televisions and networking gear as a second layer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">DIY panel work without qualification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Even with the main breaker off, the service lugs remain energized by the utility and can be lethal. Many jurisdictions also require a permit and inspection for panel work. Unless you are qualified and permitted, hire a licensed electrician; the labor cost is small next to the risk.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

