        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which whole house surge protector is best for most homes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, a Type 1 or Type 2 device with a high surge current rating, a low voltage protection rating and clear status indicators is the right choice. The Siemens FS140 and Eaton CHSPT2ULTRA are strong options. If you have a compatible GE panel with free spaces, the breaker-style THQLSURGE is the easiest install.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are panel surge protectors worth the money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, yes. The device and install typically cost roughly $200 to $600 in total, while a single replacement control board for an HVAC system, refrigerator or washer can cost several hundred dollars. Panel protection covers hardwired appliances that cannot be plugged into a strip, and the code requires it on new and replaced services.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What separates a Type 1 from a Type 2 device?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A Type 1 device can be installed ahead of the main breaker, such as at the meter, and is built for larger external surges. A Type 2 device installs downstream of the main breaker, usually on a breaker in the panel. Many current products are listed for both, giving electricians flexibility.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I still need plug-in surge strips?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for sensitive electronics. A panel device reduces surges to several hundred volts, which can still harm computers and televisions, and it does not catch surges generated by appliances on the same circuit. A layered approach, with a panel device plus quality UL 1449 listed strips, gives the best protection.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do whole-house surge protectors last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many manufacturers suggest roughly 5 to 10 years, but life depends on how many and how large the surges are. Each surge wears the internal components slightly. Check the status lights twice a year and after storms, and replace the device when an indicator shows a failed mode. Note the install date on the panel directory.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a homeowner install a panel surge protector?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Installing one means working inside the service panel, where line-side conductors stay energized even with the main breaker off. Many jurisdictions require a permit for panel work. Unless you are qualified and permitted, hire a licensed electrician. A typical install takes under an hour. Expect to pay roughly $150 to $500 for labor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the electrical code require surge protection?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The 2020 and 2023 National Electrical Code require a Type 1 or Type 2 surge protective device on services supplying dwelling units when the service is new or replaced. Adoption varies by jurisdiction, so check with your local building department. Existing services without planned work are not required to add one.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

